January first of this year a group of  individuals headed by Rothwell published  a paper in Lancet in which they  published a which they summarized a  meta-analysis of seven large-scale  studies of individuals who are taking  aspirin for protection against  cardiovascular events and overall what  was observed was that after about five  years of chronic use of aspirin it was a  very significant difference in death  rates from cancer with aspirin use  protecting these individuals by about  the extent of about twenty percent  moreover when you look cancer by cancer  you've learned to fear some cancers such  as esophageal stomach colorectal and  lung cancer all of which show quite  dramatic effects especially for  esophageal cancer and colorectal cancer  and contrast there a number of cancers  the pancreas prostate kidney blood  system in which the effects are small or  non-existent so this seems to be  somewhat tissue-specific more people  would be using aspirin if we could  somehow prevent or bypass the potential  hemorrhagic effects of using aspirin  chronically and it would be my view very  useful to try to understand whether  aspirin is actually working as an  anti-inflammatory agent or by some other  mechanism if it's working in an  anti-inflammatory way what is the  component of the inflammatory phenomena  that that is being interfered with by by  aspirin can be developed agents that  could be used chronically that that  confer the same benefit of protection  from cancer without incurring the risk  of intestinal hemorrhage let me say  something about about diagnostics here  we get into an area which is obviously  going to be very heavily influenced by  the  massive and exciting efforts that are  being made in cancer genomics but there  are a few questions that are worth  focusing on more specifically and we  know we all want to develop Diagnostics  that allow us to say which drugs  individual should and shouldn't and  should not get but some of that is  should be dependent not just on deciding  what the targets are for drugs but also  on what the what the the the pathogenic  potential of any lesion might be because  of more intensive screening and better  histopathology we're all now familiar  with a variety of neoplastic lesions  ranging from colonic polyps not shown  here to prostatic preneoplastic lesions  non-invasive intraepithelial neoplasia  the ductal carcinoma in situ is very  commonly seen in women in this country  and can be detected by mammography even  pancreatic intrepid Theo Leone aplasia  has been identified and individuals who  have been examined surgically for  prostate cancer or or for prostatitis  pack of pancreatitis sorry and in each  of these cases we are at a loss to know  the likelihood that these disorders are  going to proceed to an invasive frankly  carcinoma to state and paying more  attention to this issue is going to have  an effect not just on drug choice but  also on the general vigor with which  therapies are are pursued conversely we  need to understand more more profoundly  the significance of finding cells from a  primary tumor at basically metastatic  cited a different site and while the  alarm bells will always go off when we  find cancer cells at a distance site  we're not really sure that that alarm  bells should always go off here is an  example of a so-called sentinel lymph  node that was invaded by a ductal breast  cancer primary in the breast to  to illustrate this point because just  this year a consortium of surgeons  around the country published a paper in  which they showed that individuals who  simply have a biopsy and removal of a  sentinel lymph node the lymph node to  which the breast primarily acts as a  primary site for for grass drainage  compared to full accelerated section  which was in prior years the standard of  care for treatment of breast cancer that  did show spread of breast cancer cells  to the sentinel node as you can see  whether you simply measure individuals  who are alive after surgery that  includes includes Axler lymph node  dissection full removal of excellent  lymph nodes or just sentinel lymph node  dissection alone or whether one looks  both the disease free and alive patients  really are indistinguishable in these  two groups actually looks here as though  there's a slight advantage to only  having had the the sentinel lymph node  dissection so clearly there are  situations in which the finding of a  disseminated cell growing in another  site is not a reason to to carry out  extensive surgery a couple of words  again about therapeutics just to  emphasize a couple of points we all know  that there are traditional cancer  treatments that very often work or  sometimes derided as being cut poison  and burn but the fact the fact is that  surgery is our major defense against  cancer mortality at this point that  radiotherapy does cure some individuals  and is a tremendous benefit and has  gotten much more sophisticated in recent  years as head surgery chemotherapy tends  to be viewed with a fair amount of  disrespect even though  I would contend that it has prolonged  lives more importantly we tend to forget  that some disseminated cancers are cured  by chemotherapy alone the classical  example that everybody in this room  knows about his testicular cancer and  Laurent Lance Armstrong had metastatic  testicular cancer involving the the  lungs and brain and yet went on to win  seven tours de France and he is not  alone not roughly ninety five percent of  patients with testicular cancer can be  cured with cisplatin an agent which is  of only modest benefit in certain other  and most other cancers which it's used  the ovary long and many other organs and  we have other cancers lymphomas and  leukemias some other germ cell tumors  that can be cured with conventional  chemotherapy in my view even though  we've known this for some years we've  given insufficient attention to the  characteristics of these tumors and the  way in which these drugs work and to try  to understand more profoundly why some  chemotherapies that tend to be derided  actually are quite effective in certain  situations so there are a wide range of  things we haven't been paying that much  attention to we need to think about much  more profoundly I hope that the  provocative questions exercise will help  us to focus on some of those questions  for those who are concerned about the  availability of grants to work on these  things the NCI will be issuing an RFA  sometime in the near future to to study  provocative questions that are coming  from our website and I urge all of you  at Yale elsewhere to participate in this  community effort to draw our attention  to some of the most profound questions  in cancer research that perhaps aren't  getting all the funds and attention that  we're giving to cancer genomics and in  cancer therapeutics thanks very much
 

